Property-read hit path rebuilt: 16-byte PIC entry and kind quickening (X3a)

The register VM's inline property-read hit carried the whole cache entry -- the
prototype holder shape and the entry kind -- through the hot path, and the kind
had to be re-tested on every read to decide whether the cached slot belonged to
the receiver or to a prototype. Two changes remove that weight:

  * the hot PIC entry is now 16 bytes (shape, slot_off, kind, misses) with the
    prototype holder shape moved to a parallel cold array on the bytecode, and
    the slot is stored pre-scaled (slot*16) so the hit folds the shift away;
  * a filled OWN entry rewrites the opcode byte in place to a kind-test-free
    handler (ROP_get_field_own / ROP_get_field2_own), so the common case never
    loads or compares the kind and never sets up the prototype branch. The
    generic opcode stays as the unfilled/miss form, and the slow path rewrites
    the byte back when an entry is not (or is no longer) a plain own read.

  * quickjs.c: struct JSRegPIC shrinks to the hot half; regvm_pic_holder is a
    parallel JSShape ** on JSFunctionBytecode (allocated, freed and marked with
    regvm_pic); regvm_own_read_hit drops the kind test for the new opcodes;
    regvm_get_field_ic and regvm_put_field_ic rewrite the opcode byte after a
    fill; the two new handlers are registered in the threaded table.
  * quickjs-regvm.h: ROP_get_field_own and ROP_get_field2_own appended to the
    enum.

Bytecode version bump
  Not here, and no register-format bump either: the new opcodes are appended at
  the end of the enum, so every existing opcode keeps its numeric value, and
  they are never emitted by the encoder -- they exist only after a runtime
  rewrite, so no blob ever contains them and existing blobs stay valid.
